// @run
// The return annotation is the point — `as?` propagates nothing, so it constrains the
// enclosing signature not at all, and inference reaches `string` on its own.
// @hint redundant type annotation: `head` is inferred as `string`
// @log hello
// @log none
// `x as? T` — the trio's COALESCING member: `T` when the tag matches, `null` when it does not.
//
// IT IS SUPPORTED WHERE `T | null` ALREADY HAS A REPRESENTATION, which is a much narrower set
// than it looks, for reasons that have nothing to do with this cast. Measured on the tip:
//
//   · `string | null`  — the kind-16 nullable string ref. WORKS, and is what this file pins.
//   · `i32 | null`     — exists, but as a value NICHE (a sentinel inside the i32), not a
//                        nullable ref, so it needs its own lowering rather than this one.
//   · `Circle | null` where `Circle` is a union ARM — DOES NOT EXIST: `emitProgram: ref
//                        valtype with no interned shape`, with no cast anywhere in the program.
//   · `Shape | null` where `Shape` is a union — DOES NOT EXIST either.
//
// The last two are pre-existing gaps in the NULLABLE REPS, not in the trio, and the emitter
// refuses them by naming the missing rep. A nullable plain struct (one that is not a union
// arm) does exist, which is what makes the variant case a real hole rather than a category
// nobody built.
//
// A CORRECTION TO #1884, which pinned this as "not lowered" and said the lowering "was built
// and measured and emits the right bytes". The bytes were NOT right: the lowering derived the
// target arm from the cast node's own recorded type, which for `as?` is `T | null` — the
// RESULT, not the target. Every downstream derivation (the box tag, the atom kind, the heap)
// was therefore computed from the wrong type. Peeling the nullable to recover `T` is what the
// cast actually needed, and with it plus ONE classifier arm (`exprNullableString` seeing
// through the cast) the string case works. The claim that it was "measured" was the error:
// it had been written, not verified end to end.
type IoError = { code: i32, msg: string }
function readIt(p: string): string | IoError {
  if p == "" { return { code: 44, msg: "missing" } }
  return "hello"
}

function head(p: string): string {
  const s = readIt(p) as? string
  if s != null { return s }
  return "none"
}
print(head("f"))    // hello
print(head(""))     // none
